# architecture

- pace is a self-hostable personal dashboard that aggregates content from diverse online sources @draft
- backend uses TypeScript with Hono web framework running on Bun @draft
- data is stored in SQLite via Bun's built-in sqlite module @draft
- frontend is server-rendered HTML with Hono JSX, minimal vanilla JS for interactivity @draft
- app is deployable via Docker with a single docker-compose.yml @draft

# visual style

- UI uses a minimalist monochrome/neutral dark palette — no blue or violet accent colors @draft
- cards and panels have no left-colored borders or top-colored borders @draft
- typography is system sans-serif for body, system monospace for data @draft
- layout is responsive — grid on desktop, single column on mobile @draft

# adapters

- content sources are pluggable via an adapter interface @draft
- each adapter implements a standard interface: fetch, normalize, and return typed content items @draft
- ships with adapters for: RSS/Atom feeds, GitHub releases, HackerNews, Reddit, Twitter/X @draft
- adapters are configured per-user in a YAML or JSON config file @draft
- adding a new adapter requires implementing one file — no framework registration ceremony @draft

# llm

- LLM integration uses @mariozechner/pi-ai for multi-provider support @draft
- users configure their own LLM provider and model in the config file @draft
- LLM is used for: summarizing content items, generating daily digest, and personalized lensing @draft
- lensing means filtering and ranking content through a user-defined perspective/interest profile @draft
- LLM calls are optional — the dashboard works without any LLM configured @draft

# customization

- users can configure which adapters are active and their parameters @draft
- dashboard layout sections are configurable — users choose which panels appear and in what order @draft
- refresh intervals are configurable per adapter @draft
- summary/digest preferences are configurable: length, style, focus areas @draft

# project setup

- project is initialized with git, facts, and .gitignore @spec
